1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:14,973
At about two in the morning on the twelfth of October 1492, a lookout on a small Spanish ship saw a pale shape in the moonlight. It was land: an island in what is now the Bahamas.

2
00:00:16,073 --> 00:00:24,180
The man in command, Christopher Columbus, believed he had reached the edge of Asia. He went to his grave insisting he had.

3
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:34,112
Over the next twelve years he crossed the Atlantic four times, and opened the Americas to permanent European conquest and settlement.

4
00:00:35,212 --> 00:00:47,372
For centuries he was celebrated as a hero. Today, many see him as the man who began the destruction of the Americas' first peoples. Both views start from the same facts.

5
00:00:49,572 --> 00:01:00,793
Christopher Columbus was born in Genoa, in northern Italy, in 1451, the son of a wool weaver. He went to sea as a young man.

6
00:01:01,893 --> 00:01:16,421
In 1476 he survived a sea battle off Portugal, reportedly swimming ashore. He settled in Lisbon, the centre of Atlantic exploration, and sailed as far as West Africa.

7
00:01:17,521 --> 00:01:25,009
He became obsessed with one idea: that Asia could be reached by sailing west, straight across the open ocean.

8
00:01:26,109 --> 00:01:37,736
A popular myth says people feared he would sail off the edge of a flat Earth. In fact, educated Europeans knew the world was round. The argument was about its size.

9
00:01:38,836 --> 00:01:51,273
Columbus badly underestimated it. He reckoned Japan lay about four and a half thousand kilometres west of the Canary Islands. The real distance is more than four times that.

10
00:01:52,373 --> 00:02:02,933
Portugal's experts rejected his plan, correctly, as bad arithmetic. Had the Americas not been in the way, his crews would almost certainly have died at sea.

11
00:02:05,133 --> 00:02:21,859
He spent years seeking backers. In 1492, Queen Isabella of Castile and King Ferdinand of Aragon agreed to fund him, just months after their armies had conquered Granada, the last Muslim kingdom in Spain.

12
00:02:22,959 --> 00:02:31,492
His terms were enormous: the title of Admiral of the Ocean Sea, the government of any lands he found, and a tenth of their wealth.

13
00:02:32,592 --> 00:02:45,008
On the third of August 1492, he sailed from Palos in southern Spain with three ships, the Niña, the Pinta and the Santa María, and about ninety men.

14
00:02:46,108 --> 00:02:57,671
After repairs in the Canary Islands, they left on the sixth of September and sailed west for five weeks. As the days passed with no land, the crews came close to mutiny.

15
00:02:59,871 --> 00:03:10,708
They landed on an island its people called Guanahani. Columbus claimed it for Spain and renamed it San Salvador. Exactly which island it was is still debated.

16
00:03:11,808 --> 00:03:23,541
Its people were Lucayans, part of the Taíno peoples whose farming and fishing villages stretched across the Caribbean. They met the strangers with curiosity and gifts.

17
00:03:24,641 --> 00:03:38,081
In his log, which survives in a summary made by a later friar, Columbus noted that they had no iron weapons. He wrote that they would make good servants, and that with fifty men he could subjugate them all.

18
00:03:39,181 --> 00:03:52,429
He sailed on to Cuba and to an island he named La Española, Hispaniola. On Christmas Day the Santa María ran aground. He left thirty-nine men behind in a small fort and sailed home.

19
00:03:53,529 --> 00:04:01,657
In Spain he was received in triumph, with gold ornaments, parrots, and a handful of Taíno people he had taken with him.

20
00:04:03,857 --> 00:04:14,033
His second voyage, in 1493, was a fleet of seventeen ships and well over a thousand men, sent to found a colony.

21
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:24,221
At Hispaniola he found the fort burned and all thirty-nine men dead. They had raided local villages, and had been killed in return.

22
00:04:25,321 --> 00:04:36,777
Columbus now ruled the colony as governor. He was a gifted navigator but a poor administrator, and the colony was soon short of food and full of resentful settlers.

23
00:04:37,877 --> 00:04:49,099
In 1495 he shipped about five hundred Taíno captives to Spain to be sold as slaves. Around two hundred died on the voyage.

24
00:04:50,199 --> 00:04:56,577
That same year, his forces, with horses and attack dogs, crushed a Taíno uprising.

25
00:04:57,677 --> 00:05:12,376
Desperate to show a profit, he imposed a tribute. Every Taíno aged fourteen or over had to deliver a set amount of gold every three months, or cotton where there was no gold. Those who failed were severely punished.

26
00:05:13,476 --> 00:05:33,487
Forced labour, violence, hunger and, above all, new diseases did the rest. Estimates of Hispaniola's population before 1492 range from about a hundred thousand to several million. Spanish counts a generation later found only tens of thousands left.

27
00:05:35,687 --> 00:05:47,100
Complaints reached Spain from priests and settlers alike. In 1500 the monarchs sent a royal investigator, Francisco de Bobadilla, to Hispaniola.

28
00:05:48,200 --> 00:05:59,891
Bobadilla heard testimony that Columbus and his brothers had ruled brutally, including over Spanish colonists. He arrested all three and shipped them back to Spain in chains.

29
00:06:00,991 --> 00:06:13,279
Bobadilla's report was rediscovered in a Spanish archive and published in 2006. Its witnesses describe severe punishments, even for colonists who complained.

30
00:06:14,379 --> 00:06:20,501
The king and queen freed Columbus and restored his wealth, but they never let him govern again.

31
00:06:22,701 --> 00:06:38,723
He made two more voyages, reaching the coast of South America in 1498 and Central America in 1502. On the last, his worm-eaten ships left him stranded on Jamaica for a year.

32
00:06:39,823 --> 00:06:50,681
When local people stopped bringing food, he used an almanac to predict a lunar eclipse and told them it was a sign of God's anger. The food started arriving again.

33
00:06:51,781 --> 00:07:03,685
He died in Valladolid, in Spain, in May 1506, a wealthy man, still fighting the crown for the titles and income he believed he was owed.

34
00:07:05,885 --> 00:07:19,880
The case against Columbus began in his own century. Bartolomé de las Casas, a colonist who became a Dominican friar, condemned the treatment of the Taíno and preserved Columbus's own writings.

35
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:29,364
Critics point to the slaving, the forced tribute, the punishments, and the pattern of conquest that others followed across the Americas.

36
00:07:30,464 --> 00:07:46,763
Defenders say he should be judged by the standards of his own time, that most deaths came from diseases nobody understood, and that his voyages were a feat of seamanship and nerve. Critics reply that even his own monarchs found his rule too harsh.

37
00:07:47,863 --> 00:07:59,063
He was not the first European to reach the Americas. Norse sailors had landed in Newfoundland around the year 1000. But his voyages were the ones that lasted.

38
00:08:01,263 --> 00:08:16,559
What followed is called the Columbian Exchange. Potatoes and maize fed Europe and Asia. Horses and wheat reached the Americas, and so did smallpox, measles and, within a generation, the transatlantic slave trade.

39
00:08:17,659 --> 00:08:31,397
In the United States he became a national hero, especially to Italian Americans, who faced prejudice of their own. Columbus Day became a national holiday in 1937.

40
00:08:32,497 --> 00:08:48,668
Indigenous groups and others have long challenged that celebration. In 2020, dozens of his statues were taken down. In 2021, a US president proclaimed Indigenous Peoples' Day for the first time.

41
00:08:49,768 --> 00:09:03,229
Columbus was a bold navigator and a harsh, greedy governor. The arguments over him are really arguments about how to remember everything that began in 1492.

42
00:09:04,329 --> 00:09:15,785
He did join two worlds. But the people who had lived in one of them for thousands of years paid the first and heaviest price, and any honest account has to begin there too.
